00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.040
Like, it's, it's hard.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.550
And second thing is second thing is bloat.
You are right. Like, it's just...

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:34.250
But not bloat of temporary tables.
We need to distinguish things here.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.050
If you use a lot of deletes, Autovacuum
will ignore temporary tables, right?

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:39.540
It, it will just...

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.080
Autovacuum doesn't care about your poor
temporary tables. If you use deletes,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.200
your bloat, content, like,
as, as we all know, MVCC in Postgres-

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.120
- Yep
- deletes, delete is, is done very fast

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:02.580
because it's only marking tuple
as deleted in this session,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.960
which probably will be, like,
not so if rollback happens, right?

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.560
But then, the real, clean,
cleanup happens in, in the vacuum.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.400
Like, Autovacuum runs cleanup.
Everything is great.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:23.100
But if you delete in your table,
temporary table, Autovacuum doesn't care.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.920
It means you just accumulate
a lot of dead tuples.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.920
It's not bloat, it's dead tuples actually.
I, I, I tend to distinguish,

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.700
dead tuples from bloat
because in my def...

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.229
There's no, official definition of bloat.
You cannot find it in documentation

00:05:37.756 --> 00:05:42.436
So different people mean different
things when they say bloat.

00:05:42.436 --> 00:05:47.196
But to me, bloat is what's left
after cleaning up, dead tuples.

00:05:47.196 --> 00:05:49.696
It's empty space which is unused.

00:05:50.216 --> 00:05:55.416
It will be probably used, but in many
cases it will be unlikely used because,

00:05:55.416 --> 00:05:59.776
for example, 90% of our space is empty,

00:06:00.436 --> 00:06:04.676
and nobody will come back
and use it at all. Anyway...

00:06:04.676 --> 00:06:08.735
And yeah, and if you have index
